bf16 GEMM "K >= 3072 fails correctness" (bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log) -- diagnosed.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1), 2026-09-07, mlir-aie v1.4.2, ironenv.

WHY THIS EXISTS
================
bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log closed with "K >= 3072 FAILS CORRECTNESS -- A REAL,
UNDIAGNOSED LIMIT, NOT A TYPO" and flagged it as an open item, out of scope for
that session. This picks it back up: is it really a hard threshold, and why.

METHOD
======
whole_array.py / single_core.py (~/mlir-aie/programming_examples/basic/
matrix_multiplication/), pure-Python IRON path, same as the niche log. Two
things the original log didn't do: (1) bisect K in small steps instead of the
coarse 512/1024/2048/4096 grid, on both designs, not just whole_array; (2) get
actual mismatch data instead of the CLI's flat "FAIL! output does not match
A @ B" -- assert_close_with_benchmark's mismatch_indices option only fires on
the integer dtype branch, so for bf16/f32 output it prints nothing beyond
FAIL. Patched a local copy of single_core.py's _run_and_verify (diagnostic
print only, not committed to this repo, not persisted in the mlir-aie
checkout) to dump NaN/Inf counts, mismatch count, and sample actual-vs-
expected values before the assert.

FINDING 1: IT IS NOT A THRESHOLD AT K=3072. IT STARTS AT K/k=23 AND GROWS.
===========================================================================
single_core (default tile k=32), M=N=512, bisecting K:

  K       K/k   mismatches / 262144   note
  1408    44    0                     PASS (CLI)
  1472    46    1                     CLI reports FAIL; [341,399] actual=5440.0 expected=6016.0 (-9.6%)
  1536    48    14                    bad rows/cols scattered, all values ~9-10% low
  2048    64    10524 (4.0%)          every row touched at least once
  3072    96    262126 (99.99%)       495/512 rows fully wrong

whole_array (4 columns, default tile k=64), M=N=512, bisecting K:
  K=2816 (K/k=44) PASS, K=2880 (K/k=45) first FAIL, K=3072/4096 FAIL -- same
  44-tile-per-core boundary as single_core once normalized by k (44 in both,
  independent of column count -- the reduction loop is per-core, so this
  tracks K/k, not K itself, which is why the original log's round-number grid
  (2048 pass, 3072 fail) made it look like a hard cutoff at a suspicious
  round number when it is really a continuous function of K/k crossing ~44).

Every failing case: actual is systematically LOWER than expected, no NaN/Inf,
no wraparound/garbage -- ratios cluster ~0.87-0.90 rather than being random,
which is a precision/swamping signature, not a corrupted-address signature
(a real addressing bug -- wrong tile read, buffer aliasing -- reads as noise
or clean garbage blocks, not a smooth double-precision digit or two error
that gets worse the more terms you add).

FINDING 2: ROOT CAUSE -- THE K-REDUCTION ACCUMULATES IN dtype_out, NOT fp32.
=============================================================================
Both designs' core_fn does:
    elem_out = out_c.acquire(1); zero(elem_out)
    for _ in range_(K // k):
        matmul(elem_in_a, elem_in_b, elem_out)   # accumulates INTO elem_out
        ...
elem_out's buffer type is `C_l1_ty = np.ndarray[(m, n), dtype[dtype_out]]` --
literally the CLI's --dtype_out choice, not a separate fp32 accumulator. With
--dtype_out bf16 (what every number in bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log used), the
running partial sum is stored and re-read as bf16 (8-bit mantissa, ~2 decimal
digits) on every one of the K/k reduction steps. AIE2's aie::mmul natively
accumulates a single MAC to fp32 (docs/BENCHMARKS.md's device.yaml excerpt),
but that fp32 result gets rounded down to bf16 the moment it's stored back
into elem_out for the next iteration to add onto -- so the more reduction
steps, the more times the running sum round-trips through bf16, and once its
magnitude grows large enough that one more k-tile's marginal contribution
falls below bf16's ~0.4%-per-step relative precision, that increment is
partially or fully swamped. This is exactly the "sum many numbers into a
low-precision accumulator" failure mode, and explains both the smooth growth
with K/k (more round-trips) and the systematic low bias (swamped increments
are dropped, never added spuriously).

FINDING 3: THE FIX IS FREE -- --dtype_out f32 REMOVES THE LIMIT ENTIRELY.
============================================================================
Same designs, same K values that failed at --dtype_out bf16, re-run with
--dtype_out f32 (a real fp32 accumulator; C is cast to bf16 only once, on
readback, if the caller wants it):

  single_core,  M=N=512, K=3072, --dtype_out f32: 0/262144 mismatches, PASS.
  whole_array,  M=N=512, K=2880, --dtype_out f32: PASS, 1741.68 GFLOPS.
  whole_array,  M=N=512, K=4096, --dtype_out f32: PASS, 1830.22 GFLOPS
    (not tested past 4096 this session -- no reason from this mechanism to
    expect a new ceiling, since the accumulator is now the same fp32 the
    hardware MAC already produces per step, but not verified at LLM-scale K).

GFLOPS at f32 output is in the same range as the bf16-output numbers at
comparable shapes in bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log (e.g. 4096x2048x2048 bf16-out
hit 1846.96 GFLOPS; 512x4096x512 f32-out here hit 1830.22) -- no measured
throughput cost to the fix at these shapes.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE "FIND THE NPU'S NICHE" QUESTION
=========================================================
bf16_matmul_niche_npu.log's practical envelope note -- "K <= ~2048" -- undersells
this design. The real constraint was never K itself, it was accumulating the
reduction in bf16. With --dtype_out f32, K is not the limiting factor found
so far (tested clean to 4096); this reopens the large-K, LLM-scale GEMM shape
attention_bf16/README.md's own math said would be needed for attention to
have a chance, which the niche log had marked as blocked by this ceiling.
Still not measured: does this same accumulate-in-dtype_out pattern affect
attention_bf16's own kernel, which also targets bf16 output? Worth checking
before building a fused attention/FFN block on the assumption this is fixed
project-wide -- this session only touched basic/matrix_multiplication.

